window.__LIVE_NEWS__["2026-10-04"] = {"date":"2026-10-04","articles":[{"id":"n1791095285_0","date":"2026-10-04","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Tennessee prison chief to resign after Christa Pike's failed execution","summary":"Pike's lawyers said the failure \"goes far beyond any one person\". Pike is in critical condition after surviving two lethal injections.","body":"BBC News Tennessee prison chief to resign after Christa Pike&#x27;s failed execution Image source, Tennessee Department of Corrections By Sakshi Venkatraman Published 3 October 2026 The head of prisons in the US state of Tennessee is resigning after the failed execution of death row inmate Christa Pike on Wednesday.\n\nGovernor Bill Lee announced the change on Saturday, sharing the news in a joint statement with Tennessee Department of Correction Commissioner Frank Strada.\n\nStrada said that his stepping down is in the best interests of the state, and called the ongoing investigation into the incident entirely appropriate and necessary .\n\nPike, who was sentenced to death in 1996 for the murder of Colleen Slemmer, survived two injections and is now in critical condition at a Tennessee hospital as her lawyers seek to commute her sentence.\n\nCommissioner Strada has served with integrity, and I appreciate his willingness to put the interests of Tennesseans first during this difficult moment, Lee said.\n\nLee has ordered an independent review into Pike&#x27;s attempted execution and he halted all executions in the state for the year.\n\nAttorneys for Pike called the resignation justified but said it does nothing to help Christa now .\n\nWhat happened to her Wednesday reflects a systemic failure that goes far beyond any one person, Pike&#x27;s legal team wrote in a statement. We hope the Governor&#x27;s call for a full, independent review will expose the profound problems within Tennessee&#x27;s entire death penalty system.\n\nPike is being treated at a hospital in Nashville, Tennessee, where doctors are attempting to clear the drugs used during the execution from her body, according to legal filings.\n\nShe was injected with two lethal doses of pentobarbital on Wednesday but remained alive. She was then sent to hospital in an ambulance. While receiving the two doses, she could be heard crying, whimpering, and breathing loudly throughout the procedure , her lawyers said.\n\nAs of Thursday night, she was unconscious, intubated and on a ventilator.\n\nThe state&#x27;s Department of Correction has defended the method it used, saying the protocol does not allow for additional procedures beyond what was carried out .\n\nAuthorities have not yet said whether they will try again to execute Pike. They have also not offered an explanation for what went wrong. Her lawyers filed an emergency motion on Friday to preserve any and all evidence related to her failed execution.\n\nPike&#x27;s attorneys previously warned that her medical history and anatomy - including thrombocytosis (a blood-clotting condition) and small veins - meant there was a risk she would experience unnecessary pain and suffering during a lethal injection.\n\nThe attempted execution went ahead after multiple failed, last-minute legal challenges by Pike&#x27;s lawyers.\n\nThe US Supreme Court rejected two separate efforts to stop the lethal injection.\n\nTennessee&#x27;s governor also denied a clemency request from Pike, the only woman on the state&#x27;s death row.\n\nPike was 18 when she and her then-boyfriend, Tadaryl Shipp, beat, tortured and killed 19-year-old Slemmer in 1995.\n\nShe was sentenced to death the following year after a media frenzy around the killing in Knoxville, Tennessee.\n\nUS murderer Christa Pike unconscious and on ventilator after failed execution, lawyers say\n\nWhat happened in the failed execution of Christa Pike - and what next?\n\nI interviewed Christa Pike 20 years ago - I can&#x27;t stop thinking about her\n\nI&#x27;ve seen nearly 500 executions - but never one like Christa Pike&#x27;s\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:59 &#x27;What rollers?&#x27; - Memorable Glastonbury fan moments. 00:00:59, play video &#x27;What rollers?&#x27; - Memorable Glastonbury fan moments 0:51 How can the UK face drought and floods at same time? 00:00:51, play video How can the UK face drought and floods at same time? 1:31 &#x27;Really tough&#x27;: Badenoch on threats to politicians. 00:01:31, play video &#x27;Really tough&#x27;: Badenoch on threats to politicians 1:27 How AI can boost your chances of finding a new job. 00:01:27, play video How AI can boost your chances of finding a new job 1:27 A love story born during World War II. 00:01:27, play video A love story born during World War II 0:45 &#x27;We talk about everything&#x27; Dakota Johnson on her bff Taylor Swift. 00:00:45, play video &#x27;We talk about everything&#x27; Dakota Johnson on her bff Taylor Swift 1:08 New sniffer dog team to detect drugs used in spiking. 00:01:08, play video New sniffer dog team to detect drugs used in spiking 1:19 Have you heard about this 90 second rule? 00:01:19, play video Have you heard about this 90 second rule? 1:25 Men in court over alleged plot to target Jewish community. 00:01:25, play video Men in court over alleged plot to target Jewish community 1:22 Why students and riot police are clashing in Paris. 00:01:22, play video Why students and riot police are clashing in Paris 1:19 Photos reveal the lives of Auschwitz perpetrators. 00:01:19, play video Photos reveal the lives of Auschwitz perpetrators 1:04 Flydubai pilot describes cockpit attack by co-pilot. 00:01:04, play video Flydubai pilot describes cockpit attack by co-pilot 1:15 Teammates hailed after saving footballer&#x27;s life. 00:01:15, play video Teammates hailed after saving footballer&#x27;s life 0:42 Polanski calls for three-year cap on rents. 00:00:42, play video Polanski calls for three-year cap on rents 1:28 Biggest The Celebrity Traitors slip-up ever? 00:01:28, play video Biggest The Celebrity Traitors slip-up ever? 1:01 ‘I’d never take two weeks’: What&#x27;s leave like in NYC? 00:01:01, play video ‘I’d never take two weeks’: What&#x27;s leave like in NYC? Top stories Burnham scraps controversial plans to curb jury trials\n\nTories pledge to remove £100,000 childcare &#x27;cliff edge&#x27;\n\nChildren are dying falling into abandoned Afghan wells - this is how one five-year-old was rescued\n\nThe Celebrity Traitors connections that might surprise you\n\nAre AI models the next big danger for body image?\n\nThe murder of a journalist 25 years on - why has no one been caught?\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Another tax bombshell?&#x27; and &#x27;Terror plot targets&#x27;\n\nKemi Badenoch has cemented her image. Now she wants to put her party back on the big stage\n\nWhy Canada is preparing for a (long shot) US invasion\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nWhy thousands of couples are choosing Scotland to say &#x27;I do&#x27;\n\nA new group of celebrities have entered the castle\n\nHow to change your diet if you are taking weight-loss drugs\n\nGet your dancing shoes ready for Movie Week\n\nDrs Chris and Xand explore the impacts of having ADHD\n\n&#x27;Another tax bombshell?&#x27; and &#x27;Terror plot targets&#x27;\n\nMan shot dead as families gather for memorial\n\nThe Celebrity Traitors connections that might surprise you\n\nTories pledge to remove £100,000 childcare &#x27;cliff edge&#x27;\n\nBurnham scraps controversial plans to curb jury trials\n\nWhy Canada is preparing for a (long shot) US invasion\n\nCornell president says university &#x27;must do better&#x27; after frat house rape allegations\n\nFlydubai co-pilot attacked captain with axe, UAE official says\n\nSinfield completes final ultramarathon challenge\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c8zxl62yzxzxo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"investigation","phonetic":"/ɪnˌvestɪˈɡeɪʃn/","definition":"调查","example":"Strada said that his stepping down is in the best interests of the state, and called the ongoing investigation into the incident entirely appropriate and necessary .","_bank_example":"A BBC investigation found the accounts.","example2":"A full investigation into the crash is under way."},{"word":"commissioner","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 委员, 理事, 行政长官\\ / [法] 委员, 政府的特派员, 地方地官","example":"Governor Bill Lee announced the change on Saturday, sharing the news in a joint statement with Tennessee Department of Correction Commissioner Frank Strada.","example2":"Campaigners welcomed the move, saying it reflects growing concern about commissioner.","en_def":"a government administrator；a member of a commission"},{"word":"appropriate","phonetic":"","definition":"a. 适当的\\ / [经] 适当的, 拨出, 占用","example":"Strada said that his stepping down is in the best interests of the state, and called the ongoing investigation into the incident entirely appropriate and necessary .","example2":"Analysts point to appropriate when explaining the market's reaction.","en_def":"take possession of by force, as after an invasion；a. suitable for a particular person or place or condition etc"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"被动语态 (Passive Voice)","explanation":"be 动词 + 过去分词。新闻大量使用被动语态，因为动作的承受者比执行者更重要。注意 by 短语引出真正的执行者，常被省略。","en":"The passive voice emphasizes the action or its receiver rather than the doer: be + past participle. Used heavily in news when the doer is unknown or less important. The agent can be added with 'by' or omitted entirely.","example":"Pike, who was sentenced to death in 1996 for the murder of Colleen Slemmer, survived two injections and is now in critical condition at a Tennessee hospital as her lawyers seek to commute her sentence.","example2":"The bridge was built in 1932 and is still used by thousands of commuters every day."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"call for","meaning":"呼吁；要求（call for reforms 呼吁改革）","en":"to demand or request something publicly e.g. \"Protesters called for the minister's immediate resignation.\"","example":"We hope the Governor&#x27;s call for a full, independent review will expose the profound problems within Tennessee&#x27;s entire death penalty system.","example2":"Protesters called for the minister's immediate resignation."}]},{"id":"n1791095285_1","date":"2026-10-04","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Cornell president says university 'must do better' after frat house rape allegations","summary":"Michael Kotlikoff described the allegations of a woman who says she was drugged and gang raped as \"deeply disturbing\".","body":"BBC News Cornell president says university &#x27;must do better&#x27; after frat house rape allegations Image source, Getty Images Image caption, A student has alleged she was drugged and gang-raped inside the Chi Phi frat house at Cornell University\n\nCornell University&#x27;s president says the school must do better after a woman alleged she was drugged and raped by a group of men at a fraternity house.\n\nIn an eight-minute video statement released on Saturday night, Michael Kotlikoff described the allegations raised by the woman, identified as Jane Doe, as deeply disturbing .\n\nThe alleged incident has raised questions around the culture on campus of drug and alcohol use, and the way investigations were conducted in the case, Kotlikoff said.\n\nJane Doe was a 20-year-old student at the university when she says seven men from its Chi Phi fraternity assaulted her for hours in an incident involving alcohol and the drug ketamine on 19 October 2024.\n\nShe said she had reported the incident two weeks after it happened to Cornell University police, which investigated, but no charges were laid.\n\nLast month, Jane Doe filed a civil lawsuit against the Ivy League university, in which she alleged Cornell had failed to protect her or adequately punish the men involved.\n\nKotlikoff said Cornell was committed to investigating the way the case was handled, and said the school administration and community must do better .\n\nWe must foster a culture in which sexual assault is inexcusable and ensure our community is empowered and understands informed consent, he said.\n\nHe added that university leaders need to create an environment where victims of sexual assault feel safe coming forward and are treated with compassion and dignity .\n\nKotlikoff said it is a defining moment in the university&#x27;s history.\n\nWe must lead the way, we owe it to Jane Doe and to survivors of assault to get this right. And we must keep each other safe, he said.\n\nOn the university&#x27;s campus this week, the BBC saw growing frustration about the situation.\n\nTo play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nCornell students voice frustration at public hearing over rape case\n\nAt a public hearing for students to speak about the alleged gang rape, many told the BBC they were ashamed of what happened and said the university had failed Jane Doe.\n\nOur administration needs to change and it needs to change drastically and it needs to change fast, one student told the BBC. Something like this never should have happened, this is a disgrace.\n\nAs part of broader work that needs to happen, Kotlikoff said in his video statement that there should be a serious look at the role of fraternities and sororities in campus life .\n\nBeing part of a group can never diminish individual responsibility for our own actions, for how we treat others, or for speaking up when someone may be at risk, he said.\n\nThe university president also said that at the appropriate time , the school would speak about how Cornell will use this moment, this conversation, this anger, to help lead the broader effort to combat sexual assault not only on our campus, but beyond it .\n\nNew York Governor Kathy Hochul has said she was deeply disturbed by how the investigation was handled, and has appointed New York Attorney General Letitia James to lead a review of the case.\n\nKotlikoff said he supported the decision to appoint James to lead a criminal investigation, and welcomed an independent review by a law firm of how Cornell has handled the matter.\n\nJane Doe said in her lawsuit that she was intoxicated during the alleged incident and could not provide consent.\n\nWhat we know about Cornell University frat house rape investigation\n\nTop stories Burnham scraps controversial plans to curb jury trials\n\nTories pledge to remove £100,000 childcare &#x27;cliff edge&#x27;\n\nChildren are dying falling into abandoned Afghan wells - this is how one five-year-old was rescued\n\nThe Celebrity Traitors connections that might surprise you\n\nAre AI models the next big danger for body image?\n\nThe murder of a journalist 25 years on - why has no one been caught?\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Another tax bombshell?&#x27; and &#x27;Terror plot targets&#x27;\n\nKemi Badenoch has cemented her image. Now she wants to put her party back on the big stage\n\nWhy Canada is preparing for a (long shot) US invasion\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nWhy thousands of couples are choosing Scotland to say &#x27;I do&#x27;\n\nA new group of celebrities have entered the castle\n\nHow to change your diet if you are taking weight-loss drugs\n\nGet your dancing shoes ready for Movie Week\n\nDrs Chris and Xand explore the impacts of having ADHD\n\n&#x27;Another tax bombshell?&#x27; and &#x27;Terror plot targets&#x27;\n\nMan shot dead as families gather for memorial\n\nThe Celebrity Traitors connections that might surprise you\n\nTories pledge to remove £100,000 childcare &#x27;cliff edge&#x27;\n\nBurnham scraps controversial plans to curb jury trials\n\nWhy Canada is preparing for a (long shot) US invasion\n\nCornell president says university &#x27;must do better&#x27; after frat house rape allegations\n\nFlydubai co-pilot attacked captain with axe, UAE official says\n\nSinfield completes final ultramarathon challenge\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/ckly0leelnz4o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"administration","phonetic":"","definition":"政府；管理","example":"Kotlikoff said Cornell was committed to investigating the way the case was handled, and said the school administration and community must do better .","example2":"The latest figures show a clear link between the economy and administration.","en_def":"a method of tending to or managing the affairs of a some group of people (especially the group's business affairs)；the persons (or committees or departments…"},{"word":"responsibility","phonetic":"","definition":"责任","example":"Being part of a group can never diminish individual responsibility for our own actions, for how we treat others, or for speaking up when someone may be at risk, he said.","example2":"The incident has once again put responsibility under the spotlight.","en_def":"a form of trustworthiness; the trait of being answerable to someone for something or being responsible for one's conduct"},{"word":"conversation","phonetic":"","definition":"对话","example":"The university president also said that at the appropriate time , the school would speak about how Cornell will use this moment, this conversation, this anger, to help lead the broader effort to combat sexual assault not","example2":"The new policy is widely seen as a major step in addressing conversation.","en_def":"the use of speech for informal exchange of views or ideas or information etc."}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"间接引语 / 转述动词 (Reported Speech)","explanation":"said/told/warned/confirmed + that 从句（that 常省略）。新闻标题后正文常用转述动词引述当事人观点，注意从句语序不倒装。","en":"Reported (indirect) speech reports what someone said without using their exact words, typically with a reporting verb (said, told, added) and a tense shift: 'She said she was leaving.'","example":"The alleged incident has raised questions around the culture on campus of drug and alcohol use, and the way investigations were conducted in the case, Kotlikoff said.","example2":"She told reporters (that) the deal had already been signed."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"walk back","meaning":"收回（言论）（walk back the remarks）","en":"\"walk back\" — a common English phrase; typical collocation: \"walk back the remarks\".","example":"","example2":"The aide later walked back the controversial statement."}]},{"id":"n1791095285_2","date":"2026-10-04","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Flydubai co-pilot attacked captain with axe, UAE official says","summary":"The man accused of trying to take over the Israel-bound jet is named as Hamam al-Hammami by several media outlets.","body":"BBC News Flydubai co-pilot attacked captain with axe, UAE official says Image source, Embassy of India in Israel Image caption, The Indian government has praised captain Smit Machchhar for his immense courage during the attack\n\nThe co-pilot who attempted to take control of a Flydubai plane travelling to Israel attacked the pilot with a crash axe, the United Arab Emirates prosecutor general has said.\n\nThe flight from Dubai to Tel Aviv carrying more than 170 people plunged 17,000ft (5,200m) two and a half hours into its journey, before passengers managed to overpower the attacker and successfully take control of the plane.\n\nCapt Smit Machchhar said he was lying on the floor injured when he realised the plane was sinking and he had to act, explaining: I told myself to go for one last push to open the door from inside , which allowed the passengers in.\n\nTwo reserve pilots then landed the plane safely in Saudi Arabia.\n\nA crash axe is usually kept ​in the cockpit in case of emergencies.\n\nThe weapon used has been a key question for investigators, given the stringent security on such a sensitive route. Early reports from passengers on board had suggested it might have been a knife.\n\nThe UAE attorney general told state media that investigations were ongoing to determine the full circumstances and motives of the incident.\n\nThe co-pilot who launched the attack was an Omani national, according to Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu.\n\nMultiple media outlets including Reuters, CNN and The New York Times have named him as Hamam al-Hammami. There has been no official confirmation of this.\n\nWhile working at another airline, Oman Air, US media reported that Al-Hammami was removed from flight training because of concerns over his radical views, and was given another role at Oman&#x27;s national airline.\n\nAustralian authorities confirmed on Sunday they were investigating his links to the country, but did not provide any details.\n\nThe Australian Broadcasting Corporation, citing sources, reported he was issued a student visa in 2014 and studied in Australia between 2015 and 2017 before leaving the country.\n\nThe ABC said his student visa was cancelled in 2019 as he was not enrolled in a course at that time.\n\nCounter-terrorism officials will look at whether the man was radicalised in Australia, the ABC reported.\n\nHe then completed a three-year distance-learning course run by Buckinghamshire New University in the UK and graduated with a degree in aviation management in 2023, the university said. It added he did not take any pilot training at the school.\n\nSpeaking to Fox News, Netanyahu said that after the attacker was subdued on the flight, he called out to the crew Kill me, kill me!\n\nThe Israeli prime minister added: So, it&#x27;s clear that he was suicidal… It looks very likely that he was going to down the plane.\n\nHe also said the attacker underwent Islamist radical indoctrination .\n\nThe UAE&#x27;s attorney general, Hamad Saif Al Shamsi, said investigations into the incident revealed that the co-pilot attempted to carry out a terrorist act , according to the country&#x27;s state news agency.\n\nHe also said investigations were under way to establish the circumstances, motives and any links related to the incident.\n\nMachchhar, who was stabbed in the attack, was taken to a nearby hospital before being airlifted to Abu Dhabi, where he is reported to be in a stable condition.\n\nThe pilot, who is from India, was in good health and recovering well , the Indian embassy in the UAE said on Thursday.\n\nI am confident that the recovery will be there and I will become fit and healthy again, Machchhar said.\n\nAbout two and half hours after the plane took off from Dubai International Airport it begun to plunge sharply.\n\nThe plane signalled a general emergency code, squawk 7700, before switching to squawk 7500, a code reporting unlawful interference , or hijacking.\n\nIn an interview with the BBC, one passenger described the moment he realised that something was wrong.\n\nAlmog Itali said: Suddenly the aeroplane started to, like a rollercoaster, to drop down [with] a massive G-force. It was falling down for 20 or 30 seconds, rapidly, and then balanced, and then started once again to fall down.\n\nPeople then began to rush to the cockpit, he said.\n\nIsraeli President Isaac Herzog said on Thursday that the alarm was raised onboard by an Israeli woman, Tali Manes, who heard a commotion coming from the cockpit.\n\nHer husband, Zvika, along with three other men - Yaniv Hayun, Dr Shota Musayev and Asaf Rajuan - entered the cockpit after Capt Machchhar managed to open the door despite his injuries.\n\nHayun said, in a video released by the prime minister&#x27;s office, that the attacker was trying to destroy the instruments in order to disable the plane .\n\nHayun then described putting the attacker in a chokehold and pulling him outside the cockpit before other passengers helped to pin him down.\n\nHe added that he pulled the controls of the plane. The plane started to level out, a bit more, and then [another] pilot told me he was taking control from there, he said.\n\nIn an interview with CNN on Wednesday evening, Netanyahu said the stabbed pilot, passengers and second crew had managed to prevent a 9/11 disaster .\n\nTo play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nWatch: Flydubai pilot speaks with Indian PM\n\nWatch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:59 &#x27;What rollers?&#x27; - Memorable Glastonbury fan moments. 00:00:59, play video &#x27;What rollers?&#x27; - Memorable Glastonbury fan moments 0:51 How can the UK face drought and floods at same time? 00:00:51, play video How can the UK face drought and floods at same time? 1:31 &#x27;Really tough&#x27;: Badenoch on threats to politicians. 00:01:31, play video &#x27;Really tough&#x27;: Badenoch on threats to politicians 1:27 How AI can boost your chances of finding a new job. 00:01:27, play video How AI can boost your chances of finding a new job 1:27 A love story born during World War II. 00:01:27, play video A love story born during World War II 0:45 &#x27;We talk about everything&#x27; Dakota Johnson on her bff Taylor Swift. 00:00:45, play video &#x27;We talk about everything&#x27; Dakota Johnson on her bff Taylor Swift 1:08 New sniffer dog team to detect drugs used in spiking. 00:01:08, play video New sniffer dog team to detect drugs used in spiking 1:19 Have you heard about this 90 second rule? 00:01:19, play video Have you heard about this 90 second rule? 1:25 Men in court over alleged plot to target Jewish community. 00:01:25, play video Men in court over alleged plot to target Jewish community 1:22 Why students and riot police are clashing in Paris. 00:01:22, play video Why students and riot police are clashing in Paris 1:19 Photos reveal the lives of Auschwitz perpetrators. 00:01:19, play video Photos reveal the lives of Auschwitz perpetrators 1:04 Flydubai pilot describes cockpit attack by co-pilot. 00:01:04, play video Flydubai pilot describes cockpit attack by co-pilot 1:15 Teammates hailed after saving footballer&#x27;s life. 00:01:15, play video Teammates hailed after saving footballer&#x27;s life 0:42 Polanski calls for three-year cap on rents. 00:00:42, play video Polanski calls for three-year cap on rents 1:28 Biggest The Celebrity Traitors slip-up ever? 00:01:28, play video Biggest The Celebrity Traitors slip-up ever? 1:01 ‘I’d never take two weeks’: What&#x27;s leave like in NYC? 00:01:01, play video ‘I’d never take two weeks’: What&#x27;s leave like in NYC? Top stories Burnham scraps controversial plans to curb jury trials\n\nTories pledge to remove £100,000 childcare &#x27;cliff edge&#x27;\n\nChildren are dying falling into abandoned Afghan wells - this is how one five-year-old was rescued\n\nThe Celebrity Traitors connections that might surprise you\n\nAre AI models the next big danger for body image?\n\nThe murder of a journalist 25 years on - why has no one been caught?\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Another tax bombshell?&#x27; and &#x27;Terror plot targets&#x27;\n\nKemi Badenoch has cemented her image. Now she wants to put her party back on the big stage\n\nWhy Canada is preparing for a (long shot) US invasion\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nWhy thousands of couples are choosing Scotland to say &#x27;I do&#x27;\n\nA new group of celebrities have entered the castle\n\nHow to change your diet if you are taking weight-loss drugs\n\nGet your dancing shoes ready for Movie Week\n\nDrs Chris and Xand explore the impacts of having ADHD\n\n&#x27;Another tax bombshell?&#x27; and &#x27;Terror plot targets&#x27;\n\nMan shot dead as families gather for memorial\n\nThe Celebrity Traitors connections that might surprise you\n\nTories pledge to remove £100,000 childcare &#x27;cliff edge&#x27;\n\nBurnham scraps controversial plans to curb jury trials\n\nWhy Canada is preparing for a (long shot) US invasion\n\nCornell president says university &#x27;must do better&#x27; after frat house rape allegations\n\nFlydubai co-pilot attacked captain with axe, UAE official says\n\nSinfield completes final ultramarathon challenge\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c61wv7lgex13o?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"investigators","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 调查员；调查人员 (investigate 调查)","example":"The weapon used has been a key question for investigators, given the stringent security on such a sensitive route.","example2":"A growing number of people now call attention to investigators.","en_def":"n a scientist who devotes himself to doing research；n someone who investigates"},{"word":"international","phonetic":"","definition":"国际的","example":"About two and half hours after the plane took off from Dubai International Airport it begun to plunge sharply.","example2":"The international situation has drawn widespread attention.","en_def":"any of several international socialist organizations；a. concerning or belonging to all or at least two or more nations"},{"word":"circumstance","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 环境, 状况, 事件","example":"The UAE attorney general told state media that investigations were ongoing to determine the full circumstances and motives of the incident.","example2":"Analysts point to circumstance when explaining the market's reaction.","en_def":"a condition that accompanies or influences some event or activity；information that should be kept in mind when making a decision"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"定语从句 (Relative Clause)","explanation":"who/which/that 引导的定语从句修饰前面的名词。who 指人、which 指物，that 两者皆可；关系代词在从句中作宾语时可省略。","en":"A relative clause modifies a noun using who (people), which (things), or that (both). When the relative pronoun is the object it can be omitted: 'the law (that) the Senate passed'.","example":"BBC News Flydubai co-pilot attacked captain with axe, UAE official says Image source, Embassy of India in Israel Image caption, The Indian government has praised captain Smit Machchhar for his immense courage during the ","example2":"The scientist who developed the vaccine won the Nobel Prize."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"carry out","meaning":"执行；实施（carry out an attack 实施袭击）","en":"to perform or complete a task, order, or action e.g. \"The military carried out a series of airstrikes overnight.\"","example":"The UAE&#x27;s attorney general, Hamad Saif Al Shamsi, said investigations into the incident revealed that the co-pilot attempted to carry out a terrorist act , according to the country&#x27;s state news agency.","example2":"The military carried out a series of airstrikes overnight."}]},{"id":"n1791095285_3","date":"2026-10-04","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"Russia hits second major bridge in Ukraine's capital Kyiv","summary":"The strike on the Pivnichnyi (Northern) Bridge comes after repeat attacks on another major bridge in Ukraine's capital.","body":"BBC News Russia hits second major bridge in Ukraine&#x27;s capital Kyiv Image source, AFP via Getty Images Image caption, Technicians are repairing overhead trolleybus power lines on Kyiv&#x27;s Pivnichnyi (Northern) Bridge\n\nA Russian strike has hit a second major bridge in Kyiv, Mayor Vitaliy Klitschko has said, as Moscow ramps up attacks on critical infrastructure in Ukraine&#x27;s capital.\n\nThe attack on Saturday morning injured two people, and damaged the road surface and overhead trolleybus wires on the Pivnichnyi (Northern) Bridge, Klitschko said, causing traffic jams.\n\nIt comes after the Pivdennyi (Southern) Bridge was hit multiple times by Russian drones this week, with Klitschko warning that the enemy is tearing Kyiv apart .\n\nRussia said its drones had hit the two bridges used to transfer Ukrainian troops and military cargo. Moscow again urged foreign citizens, including diplomats, to leave Kyiv.\n\nThe two Kyiv bridges across the Dnipro River link the western and eastern parts of the capital and had not been hit before Thursday.\n\nThe Russian strikes have severed road, rail and trolleybus links across the wide river. The ongoing drone threat means restrictions on other routes, bringing traffic jams to the city&#x27;s roads for a second day - though Friday&#x27;s gridlock has not been repeated to the same level.\n\nMany of the city&#x27;s residents - which number about three million - rely on the metro to travel across the city every day . The fear is this new Russian tactic will result in sustained attacks which could effectively cut the city in two.\n\nImage source, Reuters Image caption, In Friday&#x27;s attack, a Russian drone hit cables of Kyiv&#x27;s Pivdennyi bridge\n\nWriting on Telegram on Saturday morning, Klitschko said emergency services are on their way after the Pivnichnyi Bridge was hit.\n\nTraffic on the bridge from the left to the right bank is currently blocked, he added.\n\nEarlier attacks on the Pivdennyi Bridge prompted Kyiv&#x27;s defence council to hold a crisis meeting on Friday.\n\nKlitschko warned afterwards that the city was now in a very dramatic situation as Moscow was battering its critical infrastructure, logistics, housing and other key facilities.\n\nExperts say Russian drones are unlikely to bring down the bridges - but repeated attacks will force their closure and risk weakening their structure, making them unusable.\n\nMore powerful weapons could collapse the bridges, some of which are reported to be in poor condition after years carrying heavier traffic than they were designed for.\n\nIt was reported that Ukrainian lawmakers discussed the state of Kyiv&#x27;s bridges in a recent meeting, setting out contingency plans to use ferries or temporary pontoon bridges to keep the city connected in the event of any bridges being destroyed.\n\nRussian President Vladimir Putin has said Russia is acting in response to Ukraine&#x27;s strikes on Russian energy facilities, despite the fact that it was Moscow that had first launched such attacks.\n\nImage source, Reuters Image caption, Russia&#x27;s drone strikes on the Pivdennyi Bridge caused huge traffic jams across Kyiv on Friday\n\nAfter the Pivdennyi Bridge was attacked, passengers described how dust from a blast wave entered their carriage through the ventilation system.\n\nUkrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky said Putin had decided that there were no rules now when attacking Ukraine.\n\nIn an interview with the Financial Times, he said: The Russians have failed to occupy us. So now they are carrying out massive attacks and mass killings.\n\nHe added: They want to destroy our bridges... our data centres, the internet, mobile networks, hospitals, kindergartens, schools, universities - everything. They want people to flee their cities.\n\nPutin on Thursday insisted that Russia&#x27;s attacks on Ukrainian economic targets were a direct response to Ukraine&#x27;s strikes on Russia&#x27;s oil refineries and its ships in the Black Sea.\n\nNow from every direction we&#x27;re receiving calls &#x27;let&#x27;s stop&#x27;. But they started it, Putin said after a wide-ranging speech at the Valdai foreign policy forum.\n\nThe Kremlin leader admitted Kyiv had got some results from its refinery attacks and Russia had lost about 1% of its GDP. Ukraine maintains it began attacking Russian oil infrastructure because it helps the Kremlin finance the war.\n\nRussia has targeted Ukrainian energy infrastructure, ramping up attacks every winter since Putin launched a full-scale invasion in February 2022.\n\nA sailor on a Liberia-flagged civilian ship was killed and another three crew members injured in a Russian attack on Ukraine&#x27;s Black Sea port in the Odesa region, the Ukrainian port authorities said\n\nAt least 12 people were injured in overnight Russian strikes on Ukraine&#x27;s Dnipropetrovsk, Kharkiv and Kyiv regions, local officials said\n\nTwo people were killed and three injured in a Ukrainian drone attack in the country&#x27;s eastern Luhansk region occupied by Russia, the Moscow-installed regional head said\n\nMoldova, Ukraine&#x27;s south-western neighbour, summoned the Russian ambassador after saying that five Russian drones and missiles had crossed its border and exploded near several localities in four districts . No injuries were reported\n\nRussia targets busy bridge in central Kyiv\n\nIntensified Russian strikes are tearing Kyiv apart, warns mayor\n\nThe video playlist Watch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:59 &#x27;What rollers?&#x27; - Memorable Glastonbury fan moments. 00:00:59, play video &#x27;What rollers?&#x27; - Memorable Glastonbury fan moments 0:51 How can the UK face drought and floods at same time? 00:00:51, play video How can the UK face drought and floods at same time? 1:31 &#x27;Really tough&#x27;: Badenoch on threats to politicians. 00:01:31, play video &#x27;Really tough&#x27;: Badenoch on threats to politicians 1:27 How AI can boost your chances of finding a new job. 00:01:27, play video How AI can boost your chances of finding a new job 1:27 A love story born during World War II. 00:01:27, play video A love story born during World War II 0:45 &#x27;We talk about everything&#x27; Dakota Johnson on her bff Taylor Swift. 00:00:45, play video &#x27;We talk about everything&#x27; Dakota Johnson on her bff Taylor Swift 1:08 New sniffer dog team to detect drugs used in spiking. 00:01:08, play video New sniffer dog team to detect drugs used in spiking 1:19 Have you heard about this 90 second rule? 00:01:19, play video Have you heard about this 90 second rule? 1:25 Men in court over alleged plot to target Jewish community. 00:01:25, play video Men in court over alleged plot to target Jewish community 1:22 Why students and riot police are clashing in Paris. 00:01:22, play video Why students and riot police are clashing in Paris 1:19 Photos reveal the lives of Auschwitz perpetrators. 00:01:19, play video Photos reveal the lives of Auschwitz perpetrators 1:04 Flydubai pilot describes cockpit attack by co-pilot. 00:01:04, play video Flydubai pilot describes cockpit attack by co-pilot 1:15 Teammates hailed after saving footballer&#x27;s life. 00:01:15, play video Teammates hailed after saving footballer&#x27;s life 0:42 Polanski calls for three-year cap on rents. 00:00:42, play video Polanski calls for three-year cap on rents 1:28 Biggest The Celebrity Traitors slip-up ever? 00:01:28, play video Biggest The Celebrity Traitors slip-up ever? 1:01 ‘I’d never take two weeks’: What&#x27;s leave like in NYC? 00:01:01, play video ‘I’d never take two weeks’: What&#x27;s leave like in NYC? Top stories Burnham scraps controversial plans to curb jury trials\n\nTories pledge to remove £100,000 childcare &#x27;cliff edge&#x27;\n\nChildren are dying falling into abandoned Afghan wells - this is how one five-year-old was rescued\n\nThe Celebrity Traitors connections that might surprise you\n\nAre AI models the next big danger for body image?\n\nThe murder of a journalist 25 years on - why has no one been caught?\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Another tax bombshell?&#x27; and &#x27;Terror plot targets&#x27;\n\nKemi Badenoch has cemented her image. Now she wants to put her party back on the big stage\n\nWhy Canada is preparing for a (long shot) US invasion\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nWhy thousands of couples are choosing Scotland to say &#x27;I do&#x27;\n\nA new group of celebrities have entered the castle\n\nHow to change your diet if you are taking weight-loss drugs\n\nGet your dancing shoes ready for Movie Week\n\nDrs Chris and Xand explore the impacts of having ADHD\n\n&#x27;Another tax bombshell?&#x27; and &#x27;Terror plot targets&#x27;\n\nMan shot dead as families gather for memorial\n\nThe Celebrity Traitors connections that might surprise you\n\nTories pledge to remove £100,000 childcare &#x27;cliff edge&#x27;\n\nBurnham scraps controversial plans to curb jury trials\n\nWhy Canada is preparing for a (long shot) US invasion\n\nCornell president says university &#x27;must do better&#x27; after frat house rape allegations\n\nFlydubai co-pilot attacked captain with axe, UAE official says\n\nSinfield completes final ultramarathon challenge\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c83vqxzdg1yko?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"infrastructure","phonetic":"","definition":"基础设施","example":"BBC News Russia hits second major bridge in Ukraine&#x27;s capital Kyiv Image source, AFP via Getty Images Image caption, Technicians are repairing overhead trolleybus power lines on Kyiv&#x27;s Pivnichnyi (Northern) Bri","example2":"The heavy rains badly damaged the region's infrastructure.","en_def":"the basic structure or features of a system or organization；the stock of basic facilities and capital equipment needed for the functioning of a country or area"},{"word":"restriction","phonetic":"","definition":"限制；约束","example":"The ongoing drone threat means restrictions on other routes, bringing traffic jams to the city&#x27;s roads for a second day - though Friday&#x27;s gridlock has not been repeated to the same level.","example2":"The report highlighted restriction as a key factor in the decision.","en_def":"a principle that limits the extent of something；the act of keeping something within specified bounds (by force if necessary)"},{"word":"effectively","phonetic":"","definition":"adv. 有效地, 有力地, 实际上","example":"The fear is this new Russian tactic will result in sustained attacks which could effectively cut the city in two.","example2":"A growing number of people now call attention to effectively.","en_def":"r. in actuality or reality or fact"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"现在完成时 (Present Perfect)","explanation":"have/has + 过去分词。表示过去发生、对现在有影响的动作。新闻中常用来强调\"已经发生\"的结果，常与 already、just、so far 连用。","en":"The present perfect (have/has + past participle) links a past action to the present moment. Used when the exact time is not given or the result still matters now: 'Prices have fallen sharply.'","example":"The Russian strikes have severed road, rail and trolleybus links across the wide river.","example2":"Prices have fallen sharply since January, and analysts expect the trend to continue."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"draw on","meaning":"利用；借鉴（draw on experience）","en":"\"draw on\" — a common English phrase; typical collocation: \"draw on experience\".","example":"","example2":"The report draws on data from over 40 countries."}]},{"id":"n1791095285_4","date":"2026-10-04","source":"BBC","country":"UK","title":"'Anger in the streets': Tens of thousands protest in Spain over housing crisis","summary":"More than 50 protests took place on Saturday after the government failed to get emergency legislation through parliament.","body":"BBC News &#x27;Anger in the streets&#x27;: Tens of thousands protest in Spain over housing crisis To play this video you need to enable JavaScript in your browser. This video can not be played\n\nMajor protest in Spain as millions struggle to find an affordable home\n\nTens of thousands of people have protested across Spain calling for greater protections for tenants after the government failed to pass emergency housing legislation.\n\nAbout 50 marches took place on Saturday in cities including Madrid, Valencia and Barcelona.\n\nThere is a lot of anger in the streets, a spokesperson for the Valencia Tenants&#x27; Union said. Clashes have taken place between some protesters and police in the city.\n\nThe measures put forward by Pedro Sánchez&#x27;s leftist coalition government on Friday followed public outcry over the eviction of an 87-year-old woman from her home last month after she was unable to afford a sharp rent increase.\n\nHer eviction triggered nationwide protests across Spain with protesters demanding lower rents, an end to evictions, and a ban on investment funds - or vulture funds as the government has termed them - buying property.\n\nSanchez&#x27;s defeat in Congress on Friday heightens speculation that he might call an early general election, which was not due until next summer.\n\nHis coalition government, made up of his ruling Socialist party and junior partner the Sumar alliance, does not command a majority in parliament.\n\nSpanish PM Sánchez loses key housing crisis vote after eviction of woman, 87\n\nPublished 1 day ago Photographs showed protesters in the east coast city of Valencia clashing with police officers on Saturday near arts centre Palau de les Arts Reina Sofía, which was hosting a real estate summit.\n\nSeveral protesters removed barriers and threw objects at officers, who responded with rubber bullets, Spanish media reported. Public broadcaster RTVE said nine police officers were injured and five people were arrested.\n\nImage source, EPA Image caption, Tens of thousands took to the streets of Madrid to protest\n\nImage source, EPA/Shuttershock Image caption, Tensions arose between some protesters and police officers in Valencia\n\nOne of the biggest demonstration happened in the capital, Madrid, where estimates of the number of participants varied widely. The city&#x27;s Tenants&#x27; Union said there were 500,000, while officials put the figure at 70,000.\n\nDemonstrators held banners reading There won&#x27;t be peace for rentism and I study to have a future, not to have a tiny apartment . They shook the keys they were holding and chanted: We have the keys of all the city .\n\nSome had also camped in one of the city&#x27;s main squares, Puerta del Sol, for days.\n\nOne protester, David, told RTVE ahead of the protest: We&#x27;re not here for pleasure or comfort. We&#x27;re here out of conviction.\n\nAnother protester, 26-year-old librarian Marcos Sainz, told AFP news agency that he used to share a two-bedroom flat with friends, paying 900 euros (£766) a month in rent. Today, he said a one-bedroom flat often costs 1,000 euros.\n\nSainz said he would protest against rent rises until someone dares to regulate it all , or we&#x27;ll all have to leave Madrid .\n\nSpanish police said they had arrested three members of a far-right group, some of whom had performed Nazi salutes, as they tried to reach demonstrators in the square.\n\nOn Friday night, Madrid&#x27;s Tenants&#x27; Union said that although the government&#x27;s proposed measures were insufficient, the rejection of the proposed legislation exacerbates the housing crisis which is costing the working class their lives .\n\nIt is calling for a general strike over the housing crisis.\n\nImage source, Getty Images Image caption, An encampment has been set up for days by protesters at Puerta del Sol in Madrid\n\nImage source, EPA/Shutterstock Image caption, Huge numbers of protesters rallied in Madrid\n\nThe legislation put forward in Congress on Friday was divided into two decrees.\n\nThe first included the suspension of evictions of vulnerable tenants until 2030, a two-year extension to rental leases due to expire before 2028, tax increases for tourist apartments, and a restriction on the purchase of residential properties by so-called vulture funds .\n\nThe second decree included the automatic renewal of rental contracts, a major demand of many on the left and the Tenants&#x27; Union.\n\nMPs rejected the first decree by 178 votes to 172 and voted against the second by 184 to 166.\n\nPrior to the vote, the conservative People&#x27;s Party (PP) and far-right Vox had already said they would vote against both initiatives, leaving the minority government depending on the support of smaller nationalist parties in Catalonia and the Basque Country.\n\nHowever, by the eve of the parliamentary session, the centre-right, pro-independence Together for Catalonia (JxCat) had signalled its opposition to both laws, condemning them to failure.\n\nSánchez&#x27;s ruling coalition hurriedly drew up the decree laws in an effort to defuse the housing protests that were sparked when 87-year-old Maricarmen Abascal was evicted in September.\n\nThe social backlash led to the company that owns Abascal&#x27;s flat, Urbagestión, negotiating her return to the flat on a low rent. However, protests have continued.\n\nDramatic eviction of woman aged 87 highlights Spain&#x27;s housing shortage\n\nPublished 23 September Evicted Spanish pensioner can move back home, lawyer says\n\nPublished 4 days ago The Bank of Spain estimates the country is short of 700,000 homes, based on the gap between demand and new construction.\n\nHouse prices have risen by 12.2% in the past year, according to figures for the second quarter of 2026 from national statistics institute INE.\n\nThe shortage in rental housing means that costs rose by 8.5% in 2025, according to real estate firm Idealista, with Madrid alone seeing an increase of 9.7% in rents.\n\nIt said Barcelona and Madrid were Spain&#x27;s most expensive cities to rent a home, at €23.80 per square metre and €22.70 per square metre respectively.\n\nWatch our pick of standout clips from across the BBC\n\nPrevious Next 0:59 &#x27;What rollers?&#x27; - Memorable Glastonbury fan moments. 00:00:59, play video &#x27;What rollers?&#x27; - Memorable Glastonbury fan moments 0:51 How can the UK face drought and floods at same time? 00:00:51, play video How can the UK face drought and floods at same time? 1:31 &#x27;Really tough&#x27;: Badenoch on threats to politicians. 00:01:31, play video &#x27;Really tough&#x27;: Badenoch on threats to politicians 1:27 How AI can boost your chances of finding a new job. 00:01:27, play video How AI can boost your chances of finding a new job 1:27 A love story born during World War II. 00:01:27, play video A love story born during World War II 0:45 &#x27;We talk about everything&#x27; Dakota Johnson on her bff Taylor Swift. 00:00:45, play video &#x27;We talk about everything&#x27; Dakota Johnson on her bff Taylor Swift 1:08 New sniffer dog team to detect drugs used in spiking. 00:01:08, play video New sniffer dog team to detect drugs used in spiking 1:19 Have you heard about this 90 second rule? 00:01:19, play video Have you heard about this 90 second rule? 1:25 Men in court over alleged plot to target Jewish community. 00:01:25, play video Men in court over alleged plot to target Jewish community 1:22 Why students and riot police are clashing in Paris. 00:01:22, play video Why students and riot police are clashing in Paris 1:19 Photos reveal the lives of Auschwitz perpetrators. 00:01:19, play video Photos reveal the lives of Auschwitz perpetrators 1:04 Flydubai pilot describes cockpit attack by co-pilot. 00:01:04, play video Flydubai pilot describes cockpit attack by co-pilot 1:15 Teammates hailed after saving footballer&#x27;s life. 00:01:15, play video Teammates hailed after saving footballer&#x27;s life 0:42 Polanski calls for three-year cap on rents. 00:00:42, play video Polanski calls for three-year cap on rents 1:28 Biggest The Celebrity Traitors slip-up ever? 00:01:28, play video Biggest The Celebrity Traitors slip-up ever? 1:01 ‘I’d never take two weeks’: What&#x27;s leave like in NYC? 00:01:01, play video ‘I’d never take two weeks’: What&#x27;s leave like in NYC? Top stories Burnham scraps controversial plans to curb jury trials\n\nTories pledge to remove £100,000 childcare &#x27;cliff edge&#x27;\n\nChildren are dying falling into abandoned Afghan wells - this is how one five-year-old was rescued\n\nThe Celebrity Traitors connections that might surprise you\n\nAre AI models the next big danger for body image?\n\nThe murder of a journalist 25 years on - why has no one been caught?\n\nThe Papers: &#x27;Another tax bombshell?&#x27; and &#x27;Terror plot targets&#x27;\n\nKemi Badenoch has cemented her image. Now she wants to put her party back on the big stage\n\nWhy Canada is preparing for a (long shot) US invasion\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nWhy thousands of couples are choosing Scotland to say &#x27;I do&#x27;\n\nA new group of celebrities have entered the castle\n\nHow to change your diet if you are taking weight-loss drugs\n\nGet your dancing shoes ready for Movie Week\n\nDrs Chris and Xand explore the impacts of having ADHD\n\n&#x27;Another tax bombshell?&#x27; and &#x27;Terror plot targets&#x27;\n\nMan shot dead as families gather for memorial\n\nThe Celebrity Traitors connections that might surprise you\n\nTories pledge to remove £100,000 childcare &#x27;cliff edge&#x27;\n\nBurnham scraps controversial plans to curb jury trials\n\nWhy Canada is preparing for a (long shot) US invasion\n\nCornell president says university &#x27;must do better&#x27; after frat house rape allegations\n\nFlydubai co-pilot attacked captain with axe, UAE official says\n\nSinfield completes final ultramarathon challenge\n\nCopyright © 2026 BBC. The BBC is not responsible for the content of external sites. Read about our approach to external linking.","url":"https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cm2kej47095no?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss","source_type":"live","vocabPoints":[{"word":"demonstration","phonetic":"","definition":"n. 示范, 实证\\ / [医] 示教, 实物教授","example":"Image source, EPA Image caption, Tens of thousands took to the streets of Madrid to protest\n\nImage source, EPA/Shuttershock Image caption, Tensions arose between some protesters and police officers in Valencia\n\nOne of th","example2":"The new policy is widely seen as a major step in addressing demonstration.","en_def":"a show of military force or preparedness；a public display of group feelings (usually of a political nature)"},{"word":"spokesperson","phonetic":"/ˈspəʊkspɜːsn/","definition":"发言人","example":"There is a lot of anger in the streets, a spokesperson for the Valencia Tenants&#x27; Union said.","_bank_example":"A spokesperson denied the report.","example2":"A spokesperson denied the report."},{"word":"conservative","phonetic":"","definition":"a. 保守的, 守旧的, 有保存力的\\ / [化] 防腐剂; 保存剂","example":"Prior to the vote, the conservative People&#x27;s Party (PP) and far-right Vox had already said they would vote against both initiatives, leaving the minority government depending on the support of smaller nationalist pa","example2":"The report highlighted conservative as a key factor in the decision.","en_def":"a person who is reluctant to accept changes and new ideas；a member of a Conservative Party"}],"grammarPoints":[{"pattern":"条件句 (Conditional)","explanation":"if 引导的条件从句。真实条件用一般现在时（if X happens, Y will…）；新闻中也常见虚拟条件表假设。","en":"Conditionals express conditions and results. The first conditional (if + present, will) is used for real future possibilities. Common in policy and economic news.","example":"Now she wants to put her party back on the big stage\n\nWhy Canada is preparing for a (long shot) US invasion\n\nUS Politics Unspun: Cut through the noise with Anthony Zurcher&#x27;s newsletter\n\nWhy thousands of couples are ","example2":"If the strike continues into next week, hundreds of flights will be cancelled."}],"slangPoints":[{"term":"in an effort to","meaning":"为了；以期","en":"Usual usage in everyday/news English — \"Sanctions were eased in an effort to restart talks.\"","example":"Sánchez&#x27;s ruling coalition hurriedly drew up the decree laws in an effort to defuse the housing protests that were sparked when 87-year-old Maricarmen Abascal was evicted in September.","example2":"Sanctions were eased in an effort to restart talks."}]}]};
